## Memory

Your durable memory lives at `{{ memory_path }}`.

- `short_memory.md` — your working context: recent observations and the
  current session's findings. Keep this file short; treat it as a scratchpad.
- `long_memory.md` — consolidated durable knowledge that has earned a place
  beyond the current session.
- `MEMORY.md` — index of pinned topic files; one line per entry.

### When to read
At the start of a session, scan `MEMORY.md` for relevant entries, then read
`short_memory.md` for recent context. Pull in `long_memory.md` or topic files
only when the subject at hand is covered by them.

### When to write
Add new observations to `short_memory.md`. When it exceeds ~400 lines, move
the oldest or least-relevant entries into `long_memory.md` (or break out a
new topic file, indexed in `MEMORY.md`) and prune `short_memory.md` back down.

### What to save
- Facts, decisions, or context that would help a future session resume where
  this one left off.
- Corrections and confirmations from the user — what to avoid, what to repeat.
- Non-obvious constraints, invariants, or workarounds you discovered.

Skip ephemeral details (current file edits, in-progress work, conversation
trivia). Prefer one durable note over ten transient ones.
